2026-11-28  David Walther

	* src/serve.c, boot.c, util.c, cmds.c, main.c: `9share reload` and SIGHUP.  serve and the daemon read their configuration at start, on SIGHUP and
	when the file's trust changes (a permission check each pass), not by comparing mtimes; the daemon scans for users with a file at the start,
	on a reload and every ten seconds.  export, unexport, import, unimport, mount and unmount signal the right program themselves.  serve writes
	serve.pid.  systemd/9share.service ExecReload, debian/9share.init reload.  tests/test_serve.sh: an edit is not noticed until reload.
	* src/relay.c, serve.c, cmds.c, conf.c, grammars/9share.hbnf (src/9share_conf.c regenerated): imports.  `import` blocks, `9share import` and
	`unimport`; `9share serve` runs ssh for each import with the mount's relay socket as its standard input and output; `9share offer NAME DIR`
	(run by ssh on the machine with the files) listens on ~/.9share/NAME.offer, starts a detached daud there if none runs for the request, and
	joins ssh to the carrier that daud makes with `9share carry NAME`; a newer offer ends an older one whose ssh is gone.  NINESHARE_SERVE_DELAY_MAX
	is for the tests.
	* daud/src/cmd/diod.c, libdiod/diod_link.c: --once (end with the session, or when no carrier came up within the hold time).
	* tests/test_import.sh: test_resume's read and write of sixteen megabytes with the stand-in ssh cut 25 times, with and without a secret.

2026-11-26  David Walther

	* daud/src/liblink/: the resumable link engine (link.c: frames HELLO, AUTH, STATUS, DATA, ACK, CLOSE; session ids; replay buffers; hold time)
	and SCRAM-SHA-256 (auth.c) with its own SHA-256, HMAC, PBKDF2 and base64 (crypto.c), checked against FIPS and RFC vectors.  With a secret the
	acceptor parses the HELLO but acts on it only after the proof; a failed proof changes nothing; the dialer believes no STATUS before the
	server's proof.  tests/test_link.c, test_scram.c.
	* daud/src/libdiod/diod_link.c, daud/src/cmd/diod.c: --dial, --hold, --link-secret-file.
	* src/relay.c, serve.c, boot.c: `9share relay` and `9share link` (the receiving end), one `daud --dial` per export, the mount over trans=fd.
	The secret file ~/.config/9share/NAME.secret is read for each carrier by the relay and passed to daud by serve (p9_secret_load, util.c).
	* src/cmds.c: `9share pair NAME HOST`, `9share wsl-boot --remove`; `9share setup` runs tools/9share-setup.
	* tools/9share-init, debian/9share.{init,postinst,prerm}, etc/9share.conf: the install finds the init system and starts empty.
	* man/: 9share(8) rewritten (description, fast path, then the rest), 9share-setup(1), 9share-init(8); 9share.conf(5) has hold; daud(8) the
	link options.  README, INSTALL, NEWS, docs/index.md bring the fast path first.
	* tests/: test_resume, test_resume_scram.sh, test_serve.sh, test_pair.sh, test_init.sh, test_setup.sh.

2026-11-26  David Walther

	* src/, grammars/, tests/: 9share in C.  The configuration is obconf: grammars/9share.hbnf, src/9share_conf.c generated by hbnf and
	committed (tools/regen-conf.sh).  The exporter tells the receiver its user and directory (~/.9share/NAME.info); the values are
	checked before they reach a mount option.
	* daud/: diod v1.1.0 as a git subtree; built as daud only; an attach without a numeric uid is the running user's when there is no
	user database; hostlist.c's const parameter.
	* configure.ac, Makefile.am, debian/: GNU build and the Debian package.
